{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bdea364824913a97", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tASSESSING AI-GENERATED MUSIC DETECTION IN REAL-WORLD\n2\tBROADCAST MONITORING\n3\tDavid López-Ayala1\n4\tFernando Garcia de la Cruz1\n5\tPablo Zinemanas2\n6\tEmilio Molina2\n7\tMartín Rocamora1\n8\t1\n9\tMusic Technology Group, Universitat Pompeu Fabra, Barcelona, Spain\n10\t2\n11\tBMAT Licensing S.L., Barcelona, Spain\n12\tjorgedavid.lopez@upf.edu\n13\t\n14\tarXiv:2608.07359v1 [eess.AS] 7 Aug 2026\n15\t\n16\tABSTRACT\n17\tThe proliferation of AI-generated music in broadcast media raises concerns about transparency and fair compensation, but reliable detection under real broadcast conditions remains unresolved. Existing studies report substantial performance degradation in this domain, yet their\n18\tevaluations are limited to synthetic broadcast data. To\n19\taddress this gap, we introduce BAMM (Broadcast AIMusic Monitoring), a 40-hour dataset of real-world television recordings containing AI-generated and human-made\n20\tmusic. We compare clean-trained and broadcast-trained\n21\tCNN variants across three progressively more challenging scenarios: Clean Foreground Music (CFM), Synthetic\n22\tTV Broadcast (STB), and Real TV Broadcast (RTB). Both\n23\tmodels achieve near-perfect performance on CFM but degrade substantially under synthetic broadcast conditions.\n24\tBroadcast-oriented training improves robustness compared\n25\twith clean training, although performance remains limited.\n26\tOn RTB, evaluated using BAMM, both models degrade\n27\tfurther and show substantial score overlap between AIgenerated and human-made music. These results expose\n28\ta critical domain gap and show that current training approaches on CNN-based detectors remain insufficient for\n29\treliable AI-generated music detection in broadcast monitoring.\n30\t1. INTRODUCTION\n31\tGenerative music models have rapidly lowered the barrier to music production. Their widespread adoption has\n32\tbrought to light a range of technical and legal challenges\n33\tconcerning training data provenance, training data replication, the definition of authorship, and compensation frameworks. These tensions have resulted in lawsuits against\n34\tAI-music companies such as Suno and Udio [1], as well\n35\tas licensing deals between generative-music platforms and\n36\trightsholders [2]. In parallel, policies such as the EU AI\n37\t© D. López-Ayala, F. Garcia de la Cruz, P Zinemanas, E\n38\tMolina, and M. Rocamora. Licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0). Attribution: D. LópezAyala, F. Garcia de la Cruz, P Zinemanas, E Molina, and M. Rocamora,\n39\t“Assessing AI-generated music detection in real-world broadcast monitoring”, in Proc. of the 27th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval\n40\tConf., Abu Dhabi, UAE, 2026.\n41\t\n42\tFigure 1. Generation pipeline for BAMM dataset.\n43\t\n44\tAct introduce transparency requirements for AI-generated\n45\tcontent to make AI-generated media identifiable [3].\n46\tThe need for robust detection tools has become increasingly urgent as synthetic content reaches an industrial scale. This urgency is underscored by Deezer’s report\n47\tthat, for the first time, 50% of its daily uploads were detected as fully AI-generated. [4]. Similar trends have also\n48\tbeen observed in broadcast media, with companies such as\n49\tBMAT reporting a growing presence of AI-generated music in television recordings. [5].\n50\tWhile existing AI-generated music detectors achieve\n51\tnear-perfect accuracy on isolated, high-fidelity tracks, they\n52\tare often not robust to domain shifts and common audio transformations, such as random pitch shifting, time\n53\tstretching, EQ, reverb, among others [6]. The broadcast environment introduces significant acoustic complexities: music segments are typically short, frequently backgrounded by dominant speech or sound effects, and subjected to transmission constraints. Recent benchmarks\n54\thave demonstrated that, even in synthetic mixtures, these\n55\tfactors substantially degrade performance on state-of-the-\n56\t\n57\t\fart architectures [7]. However, the extent to which AI detectors work in real-world broadcast conditions remains\n58\tunder-explored.\n59\tTo bridge this gap, we introduce BAMM, a 40-hour\n60\tdataset of real TV broadcast content specifically curated\n61\tfor AI-generated music detection. The dataset is balanced,\n62\tcontaining approximately 20 hours of AI-generated music and 20 hours of human-made music, and was built by\n63\tmatching clean references to their broadcast occurrences\n64\tthrough audio fingerprinting. The dataset contains clips\n65\tbetween 5 s and 60 s, with music appearing either in the\n66\tforeground or background. By collecting material from\n67\treal TV content aired worldwide, BAMM captures the\n68\tvariability and practical constraints of broadcast environments. We leverage this dataset to evaluate state-of-theart CNN models, and identify current architectural limitations, and thus provide a reference evaluation setting for\n69\treal-world AI music identification. The .mp4 recordings\n70\tare publicly available on Zenodo, and the baseline code\n71\tand evaluation scripts are in Github 1 .\n72\t2. RELATED WORK\n73\tThe detection of AI-generated music has emerged within\n74\tthe MIR field as part of a broader effort to identify synthetic audio, alongside related tasks such as voice spoofing\n75\tand synthetic speech detection [8, 9]. Music-specific work\n76\thas primarily focused on unintentionally left artifacts by\n77\tgenerative systems. Afchar et al. [6] showed that these artifacts introduced during the decoding stage can be detected,\n78\tunder controlled conditions, with CNN-based models, using neural audio codecs such as Encodec and DAC [10,11]\n79\tto simulate this process. In later work, Afchar et al. [12]\n80\tfurther characterized these artifacts through Fourier theory\n81\tand showed that similar patterns are also present in music generated by commercial platforms such as Suno and\n82\tUdio.\n83\tThen Rahman et al. [13] introduced SONICS, a dataset\n84\tcomposed of songs generated with Suno and Udio for the\n85\tAI-generated class and samples from the Genius Lyrics\n86\tDataset [14] for the human-made class. They also proposed the SpecTTTra family of models, demonstrating that\n87\tspatio-temporal representations are effective for distinguishing AI-generated from human-made music, achieving near-perfect performance. However, despite promising results, existing approaches remain largely limited to\n88\tcontrolled environments. Cros Vila et al. [15] showed\n89\tthat variations in sampling rate and bit rate may encourage detectors to rely on dataset-specific shortcuts rather\n90\tthan features intrinsic to AI-generated music, while models with near-perfect in-domain performance may generalize poorly beyond their training conditions.\n91\tThese works establish strong baselines, constraints,\n92\tand limitations for the task, showcasing that robust AIgenerated music detection remains unresolved in more\n93\tcomplex scenarios. One such scenario is broadcast, which\n94\tintroduces substantially different conditions: music may\n95\t1 https://github.com/DaveLoay/BAMM-dataset.git\n96\t\n97\tappear in the foreground or background and may be mixed\n98\twith speech and sound effects at different relative loudness levels. Previous work has addressed these challenges\n99\tthrough distinct tasks. Relative music loudness estimation\n100\taims to characterize how music appears within a broadcast\n101\tmix [16], with OpenBMAT providing a dataset specifically\n102\tdesigned for this purpose [17]. Audio fingerprinting, in\n103\tcontrast, focuses on matching reference tracks against TV\n104\trecordings [18], with BAF providing a benchmark for evaluating this task under broadcast conditions [19]. Together,\n105\tthese works highlight the prevalence of background music\n106\tand mixed-audio conditions in real broadcast content.\n107\tLópez-Ayala et al. [7] addressed AI-generated music\n108\tdetection in broadcast settings using synthetically constructed television content. Their study showed that existing detectors experience substantial performance degradation when music appears in short excerpts, is masked\n109\tby dominant speech or sound effects, or is subject\n110\tto broadcast-specific production and storage constraints.\n111\tThese include transitions, editing practices, changes in dynamics, and low-quality audio encoding, as further discussed in Section 4.2. To support this evaluation, they\n112\tintroduced AI-OpenBMAT, a dataset designed for AIgenerated music detection in broadcast settings. It reproduces the duration patterns and loudness relationships of\n113\treal television audio by combining human-made production music with stylistically matched continuations generated using Suno. Although AI-OpenBMAT provides a\n114\tvaluable controlled benchmark, its broadcast conditions\n115\tare still simulated through synthetic mixtures. This work\n116\textends that line of research by introducing a dataset built\n117\tfrom real television broadcast recordings and using it to\n118\tevaluate current AI-generated music detectors under practical broadcast conditions.\n119\t3. BAMM DATASET\n120\tBAMM (Broadcast AI-Music Monitoring) is a novel\n121\tdataset comprising 40 hours of real-world broadcast content curated for AI-generated music detection. The dataset\n122\twas constructed by identifying clean AI-generated and\n123\thuman-made reference tracks within a global broadcast\n124\tarchive using audio fingerprinting, followed by a multistage filtering process to retain valid broadcast occurrences. BAMM consists of approximately 20 hours of\n125\tAI-generated music and 20 hours of human-made music,\n126\tstored as .mp4 clips. Each sample captures original broadcast audio and video, preserving the authentic acoustic\n127\tdegradations and production contexts (e.g., music in the\n128\tbackground, sound effects) found in practice. The clips\n129\twere sampled from a global broadcast archive that monitors over 4,200 television channels worldwide, with all\n130\tsamples aired between January 2025 and March 2026 and\n131\tdurations ranging from 5 s to 60 s. Reflecting the technical constraints of large-scale industrial monitoring, the\n132\taudio is monophonic, sampled at 8 kHz, and encoded with\n133\tAAC-LC at bitrates of at least 40 kbps. While lower than\n134\tconsumer broadcast standards, these specifications are representative of the low-bitrate proxy streams used in global\n135\t\n136\t\findustrial monitoring, posing a significant worst-case challenge for detection models.\n137\tTo ensure trustworthy ground truth labels, we developed\n138\tan automated pipeline to bridge the gap between detecting\n139\tAI content in clean conditions and real-world broadcast.\n140\tWe first curated a reference corpus of clean, foreground\n141\t.mp3 tracks for both the AI-generated and human-made\n142\tclasses. We then leveraged an audio fingerprinting system\n143\tto identify the precise occurrences of these tracks within\n144\tthe global broadcast archive. The retrieved segments were\n145\tfiltered using a multi-stage process devised to isolate valid\n146\tclips that satisfied the technical requirements described\n147\tabove, as illustrated in Fig. 1. The following subsections\n148\tdescribe the individual stages of this procedure.\n149\t3.1 Reference Track Selection\n150\tThe construction pipeline begins with a candidate pool of\n151\tclean MP3 reference tracks. Before searching for occurrences of these tracks in TV broadcast content, we categorize them using an ensemble of five AI-music detectors,\n152\thereafter referred to as the detector ensemble. This configuration promotes methodological diversity by combining\n153\tdetectors trained on different datasets, operating at different sampling rates, and relying on distinct feature representations.\n154\tThe labels were determined separately for both classes.\n155\tFor the human-made class, reference tracks were restricted\n156\tto releases between January 2020 and December 2022,\n157\tensuring that they predated Suno v3.5 and other publicly\n158\tavailable or commercial generative music systems capable of producing music of comparable quality. For the AIgenerated class, a candidate track was included only if it\n159\texceeded the calibrated decision thresholds across all five\n160\tdetectors, requiring unanimous agreement from the ensemble.\n161\tRegarding the models in the detector ensemble. The\n162\tfirst model is a publicly available convolutional neural network presented and assessed in this paper under the name\n163\tCNN Clean. It is based on the architecture proposed by\n164\tAfchar et al. [6], was trained on publicly available data, It\n165\toperates on 5-second audio windows represented as melspectrograms computed from audio resampled to 8 kHz.\n166\tThe model weights and inference code are available in the\n167\tproject repository. For more in-depth implementation details, refer to Section 4.1.\n168\tThe second model is the publicly available SpecTTTra\n169\talpha-5s detector [13]. It was selected because it achieved\n170\tthe best performance among the evaluated SpecTTTra variants on our control dataset. The model operates on spatiotemporal tokens extracted from audio resampled to 16 kHz\n171\tand was trained on the SONICS suno v3.5 subset.\n172\tThe remaining three models of the detector ensemble\n173\twere developed for internal research purposes and trained\n174\ton in-house data. The third model is a CNN based on the\n175\tarchitecture proposed by Afchar et al. [6]. It operates on\n176\t5-second audio windows represented as mel-spectrograms\n177\tcomputed from audio resampled to 16 kHz. The fourth\n178\tmodel is a logistic regression classifier following the ap-\n179\t\n180\t2021\n181\t2022\n182\t2023\n183\t2024\n184\t2025\n185\t\n186\tH1\n187\tH2\n188\tH1\n189\tH2\n190\tH1\n191\tH2\n192\tH1\n193\t\n194\tSuno v3.5 (Summer 2024)\n195\tSuno v4 (Nov 2024)\n196\tSuno v4.5 (May 2025)\n197\tSuno v4.5+ (Jul 2025)\n198\tSuno v5 (Sep 2025)\n199\t\n200\tH2\n201\tH1\n202\tH2\n203\t\n204\t2026 H1\n205\t\n206\t0.0\n207\t\n208\t0.2\n209\t\n210\t0.4\n211\t\n212\t0.6\n213\t\n214\tAI Probability Score\n215\t\n216\t0.8\n217\t\n218\t1.0\n219\t\n220\tFigure 2. Distribution of AI-probability scores produced\n221\tby the detector ensemble for tracks released from 2021 to\n222\t2026. Score values remain low before the release of Suno\n223\tv3.5, and increase afterward, indicating the appearance of\n224\tSuno-generated music.\n225\tproach introduced by Afchar et al. [12]. It operates on\n226\tartifact-fingerprint features extracted from audio resampled to 16 kHz. The fifth model is an MLP trained on\n227\tthe same feature representation as the logistic regression\n228\tclassifier.\n229\tAll five models were trained as binary classifiers under\n230\tclean foreground music conditions to distinguish humanmade from AI-generated tracks (Suno v3.5), and all\n231\tachieved F1 scores above 98%. We performed a conservative calibration on each model to achieve a zero false\n232\tpositive rate. The calibration set included 5,000 tracks\n233\tfrom Da-TACOS [20], providing a verified human baseline that predates modern generative models, and a control\n234\tset of 500 AI-generated tracks from the clean subset of AIOpenBMAT. By deriving model-specific decision thresholds that yielded zero errors on the human-composed set,\n235\twe obtained a labeling criterion with high confidence in\n236\tthe resulting AI-generated samples.\n237\tTo further validate the selection strategy, we analyzed\n238\tthe temporal evolution of AI-positive detections among\n239\tthe artists with the highest numbers of positive cases, as\n240\tshown in Figure 2. Detection scores are consistently low\n241\tbefore the Summer 2024 release date of Suno v3.5, increase markedly after its introduction, and later decrease\n242\tover time. This behavior is consistent with the real-world\n243\tadoption of Suno v3.5 and the subsequent appearance of\n244\tnewer generative models from the same family.\n245\t3.2 Fingerprint & Broadcast Retrieval\n246\tOnce the reference tracks for AI-generated and humanmade classes were defined, we used a private implemen-\n247\t\n248\t\ftation of audio fingerprinting based on landmarks [21] to\n249\tlocate matches in our TV broadcast archive. The matched\n250\tsegments were searched in recordings aired between January 2025 and March 2026 and then extracted directly\n251\tfrom the original TV emissions. These recordings are\n252\tarchived at a sampling rate of 8 kHz and a bitrate of\n253\t40 kbps.\n254\t3.3 DMD Filtering\n255\tFollowing fingerprint retrieval, we applied a Deep Music Detector (DMD), based on the approach proposed by\n256\tMeléndez et al. [16]. This model classifies audio segments\n257\tas foreground music, background music, or speech. We\n258\tkept only clips labeled as containing music, whether in the\n259\tforeground or background, thereby removing sound effects\n260\tand incorrect fingerprint matches. These foreground and\n261\tbackground labels are also used when analyzing the performance of AI-generated music detection models (Section 5.3).\n262\t4. EXPERIMENTAL SETUP\n263\tThis benchmark examines how the training domain affects\n264\tCNN-based detectors designed to identify AI-generated\n265\tmusic in real broadcast settings. We compare two variants of the architecture proposed by Afchar et al. [6], both\n266\toperating on 5-second windows but trained under different\n267\tconditions: clean foreground music and broadcast-oriented\n268\tdata. This comparison isolates the effect of the training\n269\tdomain while keeping the model capacity fixed, allowing\n270\tus to examine the limitations of CNN-based AI-generated\n271\tmusic detectors in broadcast environments. The benchmark focuses on Suno v3.5, as model-agnostic detection\n272\tremains an open problem, and this generative model has\n273\tpublicly available data and matched reference detectors.\n274\t4.1 Shared Architecture and Preprocessing\n275\tThe CNN-based model has six convolutional layers with\n276\tfilter sizes [16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512] and kernel size 3, followed by average pooling and two fully connected layers.\n277\tIn the pre-processing pipeline, audio is converted to\n278\tmono and resampled to 8 kHz. Then the waveform is\n279\ttransformed into a time-frequency representation using an\n280\tSTFT with a window size of 2048 and a hop length of\n281\t1024. The resulting power spectrogram is projected onto\n282\t128 Mel bands spanning 20 Hz to 4 kHz, converted to the\n283\tlogarithmic dB scale, and standardized with a fixed global\n284\tmean of −4.0 and standard deviation of 3.0. The Melspectrogram is then segmented into discrete time windows\n285\tto form the input tensor of the CNN.\n286\t\n287\t19k songs. For each batch, five 5 s snippets are randomly\n288\tsampled from each track, enabling consistent training.\n289\t4.1.2 CNN Broadcast\n290\tCNN Broadcast uses the same architecture and input representation as CNN Clean, but is trained on data designed to\n291\temulate broadcast conditions by mixing music and speech.\n292\tThe music sources are the same as for CNN Clean, namely\n293\tFMA-medium for the human-made class and the Suno v3.5\n294\tsubset of SONICS for the AI-generated class. Speech is\n295\ttaken from the 360-hour clean training partition of LibriSpeech [22].\n296\tThe training set is constructed with a controlled ratio of\n297\t70% mixed samples and 30% clean samples for each class.\n298\tIn the mixed condition, speech segments are concatenated\n299\tto span the full duration of each music track and mixed\n300\twith the music signal at a randomly sampled SNR between\n301\t−30 dB and +30 dB. The resulting files are exported in\n302\tmono at 8 kHz and encoded as .mp3 at 40 kbps before\n303\tentering the shared preprocessing pipeline.\n304\t4.2 Evaluation Scenarios\n305\tTo assess the benchmark models under progressively more\n306\tchallenging conditions, we evaluate them across three scenarios: (i) Clean Foreground Music (CFM), (ii) Synthetic\n307\tTV Broadcast (STB), and (iii) Real TV Broadcast (RTB).\n308\t4.2.1 Clean Foreground Music (CFM).\n309\tFor this scenario, we evaluate the models on the clean music tracks used to build AI-OpenBMAT [7]. The dataset\n310\tcontains 476 human–AI pairs with a sampling rate of\n311\t22.05 kHz and a bitrate of 353 kbps. The AI tracks were\n312\tgenerated from their corresponding human tracks using\n313\tSuno’s extend function. As a result, the pairs are closely\n314\tmatched in musical characteristics such as style, timbre,\n315\tpitch, and key. This scenario provides a direct point of\n316\tcomparison with the synthetic broadcast scenario, which\n317\tis built from the same source material.\n318\t4.2.2 Synthetic TV Broadcast (STB).\n319\tWe evaluate the synthetic broadcast scenario using the full\n320\tAI-OpenBMAT dataset, following [7]. The dataset contains 3,294 tracks, totaling 54.9 hours of synthetic broadcast material, with a sampling rate of 22.05 kHz and a bitrate of 353 kbps. These data were created from the same\n321\tclean foreground references described above, using controlled mixtures designed to accurately emulate broadcast\n322\tconditions. This scenario allows us to evaluate model performance under synthetic broadcast conditions while keeping the underlying musical material controlled.\n323\t\n324\t4.1.1 CNN Clean\n325\t\n326\t4.2.3 Real TV Broadcast (RTB).\n327\t\n328\tCNN Clean is trained on clean foreground music only. Audio is converted to mono and resampled to 8 kHz in the\n329\tpre-processing pipeline. The human-made class is drawn\n330\tfrom the FMA-medium dataset, comprising approximately\n331\t25k songs, while the AI-generated class is built from the\n332\tSuno v3.5 subset of SONICS, comprising approximately\n333\t\n334\tThe real broadcast scenario uses the full BAMM dataset\n335\tintroduced in this work. It comprises 40 hours of real\n336\tTV broadcast recordings, with approximately 20 hours per\n337\tclass, at a sampling rate of 8 kHz and a bitrate of 40 kbps\n338\tusing AAC-LC encoding. This setting extends previous\n339\tbroadcast-oriented evaluations by shifting from synthetic\n340\t\n341\t\fScenario\n342\t\n343\tCFM\n344\tSTB\n345\tRTB\n346\t\n347\tCNN Clean\n348\t\n349\tCNN Broadcast\n350\t\n351\tF1\n352\t\n353\tROC\n354\t\n355\tF1\n356\t\n357\tROC\n358\t\n359\t0.992\n360\t0.342\n361\t0.186\n362\t\n363\t0.998\n364\t0.909\n365\t0.707\n366\t\n367\t0.992\n368\t0.661\n369\t0.472\n370\t\n371\t0.996\n372\t0.926\n373\t0.775\n374\t\n375\tTable 1. Results across the three evaluation scenarios:\n376\tClean Foreground Music (CFM), Synthetic TV Broadcast\n377\t(STB), and Real TV Broadcast (RTB).\n378\tapproximations to real television content, enabling the\n379\tmodels to be tested under the conditions of the target environment.\n380\t5. RESULTS\n381\tWe evaluate both models across three scenarios with increasing levels of broadcast complexity: Clean Foreground Music (CFM), Synthetic TV Broadcast (STB), and\n382\tReal TV Broadcast (RTB). The comparison between CNN\n383\tClean and CNN Broadcast allows us to analyze how the\n384\ttraining domain influences the robustness of AI-generated\n385\tmusic detection. While the CFM scenario provides a controlled evaluation of artifact detection, the STB setup introduces synthetic broadcast conditions through controlled\n386\tmusic–speech mixtures. Finally, the RTB case evaluates\n387\tboth models on the BAMM dataset introduced in this work,\n388\tcapturing the challenges of real television content.\n389\t5.1 Evaluation on Clean Foreground Music\n390\tThe first stage of this evaluation aims to verify whether detectors trained under real-world broadcast constraints, i.e.,\n391\tlow-quality audio at 8 kHz sampling rate, remain reliable\n392\tfor detecting AI-generated music under clean, high-quality\n393\tforeground conditions. As shown in Table 1, both CNN\n394\tClean and CNN Broadcast achieve F1-scores above 99%,\n395\tdemonstrating that the artifacts learned from low-quality\n396\ttraining data are still present in the high-quality data typically used for this task and remain detectable even when\n397\tconsidering only the lower-frequency components of the\n398\taudio spectrum. These results validate the proposed models and provide a baseline for the subsequent evaluations.\n399\t5.2 Evaluation on Synthetic TV Broadcast\n400\tThe synthetic TV broadcast (STB) scenario represents the\n401\tfirst evaluation under mixed-audio conditions, in which\n402\tAI-generated music is no longer isolated but combined\n403\twith speech. As observed in Table 1, the transition from\n404\tclean foreground music to synthetic broadcast conditions\n405\tproduces a significant reduction in detection performance\n406\tfor both models. The clean-trained model suffers the\n407\tgreatest degradation, demonstrating that artifacts learned\n408\tfrom isolated music do not fully transfer when partially\n409\tmasked by the surrounding broadcast context. This result is consistent with the performance reported for AIOpenBMAT [7] and validates the experimental setup. The\n410\t\n411\tCNN Broadcast model alleviates this degradation by learning from similar mixed conditions, demonstrating that exposure to broadcast-like data improves artifact detection\n412\tunder masking. However, its limited performance suggests\n413\tthat the problem is not only related to the training distribution but also to an intrinsic reduction in artifact visibility\n414\tarising from the broadcast scenario’s mixture process.\n415\t5.3 Evaluation on Real TV Broadcast\n416\tThe RTB scenario is the most challenging setting in this\n417\twork, as it evaluates the detectors directly on real television\n418\tcontent. Unlike STB, which approximates broadcast conditions using controlled synthetic mixtures, RTB includes\n419\tthe full complexity of real recordings. As shown in Table 1, both models perform worse than in STB, indicating\n420\tthat the synthetic setting does not fully capture real broadcast conditions.\n421\tThe CNN Clean model shows the largest degradation,\n422\tconfirming that artifacts learned from isolated foreground\n423\tmusic do not transfer reliably to real broadcasts. In addition to the reduced audio quality of the RTB recordings\n424\t(8 kHz sampling rate and 40 kbps AAC-LC), sound effects, transitions, and diverse mixing strategies can further\n425\tobscure AI-related artifacts.\n426\tFigure 3 shows a detailed comparison of the models’\n427\tperformance when considering BAMM clips with music\n428\tin the background or in the foreground. Both models retain some discriminative ability and perform better on foreground than on background music. This confirms that\n429\tmasking is a major source of degradation. However, the\n430\tlimited performance on foreground segments also indicates\n431\tthat degraded audio quality and other broadcast sounds affect artifact visibility.\n432\tThe score distributions in Figure 4 show that the main\n433\tdifficulty is detecting AI-generated music rather than rejecting human content. While human samples are generally assigned scores near zero, many AI samples also receive low scores, particularly in background music. Overall, broadcast-simulated training improves robustness, but\n434\tit does not fully overcome the effects of real broadcast conditions.\n435\t6. CONCLUSIONS\n436\tIn this paper, we introduced BAMM, a dataset for AIgenerated music detection designed to address key limitations of existing benchmarks. Unlike datasets focused\n437\tprimarily on clean or isolated musical excerpts, BAMM\n438\ttargets the real broadcast setting, where music appears in\n439\tshort segments, alternates between foreground and background roles, and is mixed with speech and sound effects.\n440\tOur experiments show that CNN-based detectors\n441\ttrained on clean foreground music or simulated broadcast\n442\tdata retain some discriminative capacity under controlled\n443\tconditions, but their performance degrades substantially as\n444\tthe evaluation scenario becomes more realistic. In particular, although performance remains limited for both foreground and background music, the models perform slightly\n445\t\n446\t\fAll Clips\n447\t\n448\tBackground music\n449\t\n450\tForeground music\n451\t\n452\t1.0\n453\t\n454\t1.0\n455\t\n456\t0.8\n457\t\n458\t0.8\n459\t\n460\t0.8\n461\t\n462\t0.6\n463\t\n464\t0.6\n465\t\n466\t0.6\n467\t\n468\t0.4\n469\t\n470\t0.4\n471\t\n472\t0.4\n473\t\n474\tTrue Positive Rate\n475\t\n476\t1.0\n477\t\n478\t0.2\n479\t\n480\t0.2\n481\t\n482\t0.2\n483\t\n484\t0.0\n485\t\n486\tCNN Broadcast (AUC=0.775)\n487\tCNN Clean (AUC=0.707)\n488\tRandom\n489\t0.0\n490\t\n491\tCNN Broadcast (AUC=0.858)\n492\tCNN Clean (AUC=0.782)\n493\tRandom\n494\t0.0\n495\t\n496\t0.0\n497\t\n498\t0.2\n499\t\n500\t0.4\n501\t\n502\t0.6\n503\t\n504\t0.8\n505\t\n506\tFalse Positive Rate\n507\t\n508\t1.0\n509\t\n510\t0.0\n511\t\n512\t0.2\n513\t\n514\t0.4\n515\t\n516\t0.6\n517\t\n518\t0.8\n519\t\n520\tFalse Positive Rate\n521\t\n522\t1.0\n523\t\n524\tCNN Broadcast (AUC=0.745)\n525\tCNN Clean (AUC=0.667)\n526\tRandom\n527\t\n528\t0.0\n529\t\n530\t0.2\n531\t\n532\t0.4\n533\t\n534\t0.6\n535\t\n536\t0.8\n537\t\n538\tFalse Positive Rate\n539\t\n540\t1.0\n541\t\n542\tFigure 3. ROC curves for the clean-trained and broadcast-trained CNN models when evaluated on all BAMM clips, only\n543\ton foreground music, and only on background music. Performance decreases for background music, showing that detection\n544\tbecomes more difficult when the musical signal is less salient or mixed with other content.\n545\t\n546\tCNN Clean\n547\t1400\n548\t1200\n549\t1000\n550\t800\n551\t600\n552\t400\n553\t200\n554\t0\n555\t\n556\t1200\n557\t\n558\tHuman\n559\tAI\n560\t1000\n561\t\n562\tMusic\n563\tBackground music\n564\t\n565\tHuman\n566\tAI\n567\t\n568\t800\n569\t600\n570\t400\n571\t\n572\tretaría de Estado de Digitalización e Inteligencia\n573\tArtificial, the European Union-Next Generation EU\n574\tfunds and BMAT Music Innovators.\n575\tAnd by the\n576\t\"IMPA\" project (PID2023-152250OB-I00) funded by\n577\tMCIU/AEI/10.13039/501100011033/FEDER, UE.\n578\t\n579\t200\n580\t0\n581\t\n582\tCNN Clean\n583\t\n584\t2500\n585\t\n586\tHuman 2000\n587\tAI\n588\t1750\n589\t\n590\t2000\n591\t1500\n592\t1000\n593\t500\n594\t0 0.0\n595\t\n596\tCNN Broadcast\n597\t\n598\t0.2\n599\t\n600\t0.4\n601\t\n602\t0.6\n603\t\n604\t0.8\n605\t\n606\tProbability (AI)\n607\t\n608\t1500\n609\t1250\n610\t1000\n611\t750\n612\t500\n613\t250\n614\t0 0.0\n615\t1.0\n616\t\n617\tCNN Broadcast\n618\t\n619\t0.2\n620\t\n621\t0.4\n622\t\n623\t0.6\n624\t\n625\t0.8\n626\t\n627\tProbability (AI)\n628\t\n629\t8. REFERENCES\n630\tHuman\n631\tAI\n632\t\n633\t1.0\n634\t\n635\tFigure 4. Score distributions for the CNN models under\n636\tReal TV Broadcast conditions. Blue and red densities denote human-made and AI-generated clips.\n637\tbetter when the music is in the foreground. This suggests that current detectors are not reliably capturing AIgenerated music artifacts, and that the task becomes even\n638\tmore difficult when the musical signal is less salient or embedded within complex audio mixtures.\n639\tThese findings highlight the need for more robust detection methods capable of modeling the acoustic and contextual complexity of real broadcast media. BAMM provides\n640\ta benchmark for this direction by exposing failure modes\n641\tnot fully captured by clean-music datasets and encouraging\n642\tthe development of models that can detect AI-generated\n643\tmusic in challenging, real-world broadcast environments.\n644\t\n645\t[1] D. Tencer, “Major record companies sue Suno,\n646\tUdio for ‘mass infringement’ of copyright,”\n647\thttps://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/majorrecord-companies-sue-ai-music-generators-suno-udiofor-mass-infringement-of-copyright/, Jun. 2024.\n648\t[2] M. Stassen, “Warner Music Group strikes\n649\t‘landmark’ deal with Suno;\n650\tsettles copyright lawsuit against AI music generator,”\n651\thttps://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/warnermusic-group-settles-with-suno-strikes-first-of-itskind-deal-with-ai-song-generator/, Nov. 2025.\n652\t[3] X. Serra, R. O. Araz, R. Batlle-Roca, L. Juvela,\n653\tD. López, and M. Rocamora, “Technical Solutions\n654\tfor Marking and Detecting AI-generated Audio in\n655\tthe Context of Article 50 of the AI Act,” European Commission, Technical Report, 2026, tender ECCNECT/2024/VLVP/0115 Watermarking Audio.\n656\t[4] J. Wendel, “Ai music tops 50% of daily uploads\n657\ton deezer,” https://newsroom-deezer.com/2026/07/\n658\tai-music-exceeds-50-percent-daily-uploads-deezer/,\n659\t2026, [Online; accessed 24-July-2026].\n660\t\n661\t7. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\n662\t\n663\t[5] D. Bolboacă, “First, video killed the radio star, and\n664\tnow AI is going after video: A study on detecting GenAI music in broadcast audio,” https://www.\n665\tbmat.com/genai-music-broadcasting/, 2026, [Online;\n666\taccessed 24-July-2026].\n667\t\n668\tThis work is supported by the \"Cátedra IA y Música\"\n669\tproject (TSI-100929-2023-1), funded by the Sec-\n670\t\n671\t[6] D. Afchar, G. Meseguer-Brocal, and R. Hennequin,\n672\t“AI-generated music detection and its challenges,” in\n673\t\n674\t\fProc. of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2025.\n675\t[7] D. López-Ayala, A. Cabello, P. Zinemanas, E. Molina,\n676\tand M. Rocamora, “AI-generated music detection in\n677\tbroadcast monitoring,” in Proc. of the 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\n678\tProcessing (ICASSP), 2026.\n679\t[8] Y. Li, M. Milling, L. Specia, and B. W. Schuller, “From\n680\tAudio Deepfake Detection to AI-Generated Music Detection – a pathway and overview,” arXiv preprint\n681\tarXiv:2412.00571, 2024.\n682\t[9] D. Salvi, H. V. Koops, and E. Quinton, “Not All Deepfakes Are Created Equal: Triaging Audio Forgeries for\n683\tRobust Deepfake Singer Identification,” arXiv preprint\n684\tarXiv:2510.17474, 2025.\n685\t[10] A. Défossez, J. Copet, G. Synnaeve, and Y. Adi, “High\n686\tfidelity neural audio compression,” Transactions on\n687\tMachine Learning Research (TMLR), 2023.\n688\t[11] R. Kumar, P. Seetharaman, A. Luebs, I. Kumar, and\n689\tK. Kumar, “High-fidelity audio compression with improved RVQGAN,” in Proc. of the Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS), 2023.\n690\t[12] D. Afchar, G. Meseguer-Brocal, K. Akesbi, and\n691\tR. Hennequin, “A Fourier Explanation of AI-music\n692\tArtifacts,” in Proceedings of the International Society\n693\tfor Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR),\n694\t2025.\n695\t[13] M. A. Rahman, Z. I. A. Hakim, N. H. Sarker, B. Paul,\n696\tand S. A. Fattah, “Sonics: Synthetic or not - identifying counterfeit songs,” in International Conference on\n697\tLearning Representations (ICLR), 2025.\n698\t[14] C.\n699\tGDCJ,\n700\t“Genius\n701\tsong\n702\tlyrics,”\n703\thttps:\n704\t//www.kaggle.com/datasets/carlosgdcj/\n705\tgenius-song-lyrics-with-language-information,\n706\t2023, [Online; accessed 16-March-2026].\n707\t[15] L. C. Vila, B. L. T. Sturm, L. Casini, and D. Dalmazzo, “The AI Music Arms Race: On the Detection of AI-Generated Music,” Transactions of the International Society for Music Information Retrieval\n708\t(TISMIR), vol. 8, no. 1, pp. 179–194, 2025.\n709\t[16] B. Meléndez Catalán, “Relative music loudness estimation in TV broadcast audio using deep learning: An\n710\tindustrial perspective,” Ph.D. dissertation, Universitat\n711\tPompeu Fabra, Apr. 2021.\n712\t[17] B. Meléndez-Catalán, E. Molina, and E. Gómez,\n713\t“Open broadcast media audio from tv: A dataset of\n714\ttv broadcast audio with relative music loudness annotations,” Transactions of the International Society for\n715\tMusic Information Retrieval (TISMIR), vol. 2, no. 1,\n716\tpp. 43–51, 2019.\n717\t\n718\t[18] G. Cortès-Sebastià, M. Miron, E. Molina, A. Ciurana,\n719\tand X. Serra, “Enhanced television broadcast monitoring with source separation-assisted audio fingerprinting: A case study,” Multimedia Tools and Applications,\n720\tvol. 84, no. 42, pp. 50 595–50 628, 2025.\n721\t[19] G. Cortès, A. Ciurana, E. Molina, M. Miron, O. Meyers, J. Six, and X. Serra, “BAF: An audio fingerprinting dataset for broadcast monitoring,” in Proc. of the\n722\tInternational Society for Music Information Retrieval\n723\tConference (ISMIR), 2022.\n724\t[20] F. Yesiler, C. Tralie, A. Correya, D. F. Silva, P. Tovstogan, E. Gómez, and X. Serra, “Da-TACOS: A dataset\n725\tfor cover song identification and understanding,” in\n726\tProc. of the International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2019.\n727\t[21] G. Cortès Sebastià, “Music identification with audio\n728\tfingerprinting an industrial perspective,” Ph.D. dissertation, Universitat Pompeu Fabra, Feb. 2025.\n729\t[22] M. Korvas, O. Plátek, O. Dušek, L. Žilka, and F. Jurčíček, “Free English and Czech telephone speech corpus shared under the CC-BY-SA 3.0 license,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Language\n730\tResources and Evaluation (LREC), 2014.\n731\t\n732\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "9c9ac76b-e130-4037-8c17-d753c828eda5", "uuid": "85cf9314-3334-4d97-9bbc-78af14824491", "timestamp": "2026-08-10T06:28:01.781Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608.07359.txt", "content": "ASSESSING AI-GENERATED MUSIC DETECTION IN REAL-WORLD\nBROADCAST MONITORING\nDavid López-Ayala1\nFernando Garcia de la Cruz1\nPablo Zinemanas2\nEmilio Molina2\nMartín Rocamora1\n1\nMusic Technology Group, Universitat Pompeu Fabra, Barcelona, Spain\n2\nBMAT Licensing S.L., Barcelona, Spain\njorgedavid.lopez@upf.edu\n\narXiv:2608.07359v1 [eess.AS] 7 Aug 2026\n\nABSTRACT\nThe proliferation of AI-generated music in broadcast media raises concerns about transparency and fair compensation, but reliable detection under real broadcast conditions remains unresolved. Existing studies report substantial performance degradation in this domain, yet their\nevaluations are limited to synthetic broadcast data. To\naddress this gap, we introduce BAMM (Broadcast AIMusic Monitoring), a 40-hour dataset of real-world television recordings containing AI-generated and human-made\nmusic. We compare clean-trained and broadcast-trained\nCNN variants across three progressively more challenging scenarios: Clean Foreground Music (CFM), Synthetic\nTV Broadcast (STB), and Real TV Broadcast (RTB). Both\nmodels achieve near-perfect performance on CFM but degrade substantially under synthetic broadcast conditions.\nBroadcast-oriented training improves robustness compared\nwith clean training, although performance remains limited.\nOn RTB, evaluated using BAMM, both models degrade\nfurther and show substantial score overlap between AIgenerated and human-made music. These results expose\na critical domain gap and show that current training approaches on CNN-based detectors remain insufficient for\nreliable AI-generated music detection in broadcast monitoring.\n1. INTRODUCTION\nGenerative music models have rapidly lowered the barrier to music production. Their widespread adoption has\nbrought to light a range of technical and legal challenges\nconcerning training data provenance, training data replication, the definition of authorship, and compensation frameworks. These tensions have resulted in lawsuits against\nAI-music companies such as Suno and Udio [1], as well\nas licensing deals between generative-music platforms and\nrightsholders [2]. In parallel, policies such as the EU AI\n© D. López-Ayala, F. Garcia de la Cruz, P Zinemanas, E\nMolina, and M. Rocamora. Licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0). Attribution: D. LópezAyala, F. Garcia de la Cruz, P Zinemanas, E Molina, and M. Rocamora,\n“Assessing AI-generated music detection in real-world broadcast monitoring”, in Proc. of the 27th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval\nConf., Abu Dhabi, UAE, 2026.\n\nFigure 1. Generation pipeline for BAMM dataset.\n\nAct introduce transparency requirements for AI-generated\ncontent to make AI-generated media identifiable [3].\nThe need for robust detection tools has become increasingly urgent as synthetic content reaches an industrial scale. This urgency is underscored by Deezer’s report\nthat, for the first time, 50% of its daily uploads were detected as fully AI-generated. [4]. Similar trends have also\nbeen observed in broadcast media, with companies such as\nBMAT reporting a growing presence of AI-generated music in television recordings. [5].\nWhile existing AI-generated music detectors achieve\nnear-perfect accuracy on isolated, high-fidelity tracks, they\nare often not robust to domain shifts and common audio transformations, such as random pitch shifting, time\nstretching, EQ, reverb, among others [6]. The broadcast environment introduces significant acoustic complexities: music segments are typically short, frequently backgrounded by dominant speech or sound effects, and subjected to transmission constraints. Recent benchmarks\nhave demonstrated that, even in synthetic mixtures, these\nfactors substantially degrade performance on state-of-the-\n\n\fart architectures [7]. However, the extent to which AI detectors work in real-world broadcast conditions remains\nunder-explored.\nTo bridge this gap, we introduce BAMM, a 40-hour\ndataset of real TV broadcast content specifically curated\nfor AI-generated music detection. The dataset is balanced,\ncontaining approximately 20 hours of AI-generated music and 20 hours of human-made music, and was built by\nmatching clean references to their broadcast occurrences\nthrough audio fingerprinting. The dataset contains clips\nbetween 5 s and 60 s, with music appearing either in the\nforeground or background. By collecting material from\nreal TV content aired worldwide, BAMM captures the\nvariability and practical constraints of broadcast environments. We leverage this dataset to evaluate state-of-theart CNN models, and identify current architectural limitations, and thus provide a reference evaluation setting for\nreal-world AI music identification. The .mp4 recordings\nare publicly available on Zenodo, and the baseline code\nand evaluation scripts are in Github 1 .\n2. RELATED WORK\nThe detection of AI-generated music has emerged within\nthe MIR field as part of a broader effort to identify synthetic audio, alongside related tasks such as voice spoofing\nand synthetic speech detection [8, 9]. Music-specific work\nhas primarily focused on unintentionally left artifacts by\ngenerative systems. Afchar et al. [6] showed that these artifacts introduced during the decoding stage can be detected,\nunder controlled conditions, with CNN-based models, using neural audio codecs such as Encodec and DAC [10,11]\nto simulate this process. In later work, Afchar et al. [12]\nfurther characterized these artifacts through Fourier theory\nand showed that similar patterns are also present in music generated by commercial platforms such as Suno and\nUdio.\nThen Rahman et al. [13] introduced SONICS, a dataset\ncomposed of songs generated with Suno and Udio for the\nAI-generated class and samples from the Genius Lyrics\nDataset [14] for the human-made class. They also proposed the SpecTTTra family of models, demonstrating that\nspatio-temporal representations are effective for distinguishing AI-generated from human-made music, achieving near-perfect performance. However, despite promising results, existing approaches remain largely limited to\ncontrolled environments. Cros Vila et al. [15] showed\nthat variations in sampling rate and bit rate may encourage detectors to rely on dataset-specific shortcuts rather\nthan features intrinsic to AI-generated music, while models with near-perfect in-domain performance may generalize poorly beyond their training conditions.\nThese works establish strong baselines, constraints,\nand limitations for the task, showcasing that robust AIgenerated music detection remains unresolved in more\ncomplex scenarios. One such scenario is broadcast, which\nintroduces substantially different conditions: music may\n1 https://github.com/DaveLoay/BAMM-dataset.git\n\nappear in the foreground or background and may be mixed\nwith speech and sound effects at different relative loudness levels. Previous work has addressed these challenges\nthrough distinct tasks. Relative music loudness estimation\naims to characterize how music appears within a broadcast\nmix [16], with OpenBMAT providing a dataset specifically\ndesigned for this purpose [17]. Audio fingerprinting, in\ncontrast, focuses on matching reference tracks against TV\nrecordings [18], with BAF providing a benchmark for evaluating this task under broadcast conditions [19]. Together,\nthese works highlight the prevalence of background music\nand mixed-audio conditions in real broadcast content.\nLópez-Ayala et al. [7] addressed AI-generated music\ndetection in broadcast settings using synthetically constructed television content. Their study showed that existing detectors experience substantial performance degradation when music appears in short excerpts, is masked\nby dominant speech or sound effects, or is subject\nto broadcast-specific production and storage constraints.\nThese include transitions, editing practices, changes in dynamics, and low-quality audio encoding, as further discussed in Section 4.2. To support this evaluation, they\nintroduced AI-OpenBMAT, a dataset designed for AIgenerated music detection in broadcast settings. It reproduces the duration patterns and loudness relationships of\nreal television audio by combining human-made production music with stylistically matched continuations generated using Suno. Although AI-OpenBMAT provides a\nvaluable controlled benchmark, its broadcast conditions\nare still simulated through synthetic mixtures. This work\nextends that line of research by introducing a dataset built\nfrom real television broadcast recordings and using it to\nevaluate current AI-generated music detectors under practical broadcast conditions.\n3. BAMM DATASET\nBAMM (Broadcast AI-Music Monitoring) is a novel\ndataset comprising 40 hours of real-world broadcast content curated for AI-generated music detection. The dataset\nwas constructed by identifying clean AI-generated and\nhuman-made reference tracks within a global broadcast\narchive using audio fingerprinting, followed by a multistage filtering process to retain valid broadcast occurrences. BAMM consists of approximately 20 hours of\nAI-generated music and 20 hours of human-made music,\nstored as .mp4 clips. Each sample captures original broadcast audio and video, preserving the authentic acoustic\ndegradations and production contexts (e.g., music in the\nbackground, sound effects) found in practice. The clips\nwere sampled from a global broadcast archive that monitors over 4,200 television channels worldwide, with all\nsamples aired between January 2025 and March 2026 and\ndurations ranging from 5 s to 60 s. Reflecting the technical constraints of large-scale industrial monitoring, the\naudio is monophonic, sampled at 8 kHz, and encoded with\nAAC-LC at bitrates of at least 40 kbps. While lower than\nconsumer broadcast standards, these specifications are representative of the low-bitrate proxy streams used in global\n\n\findustrial monitoring, posing a significant worst-case challenge for detection models.\nTo ensure trustworthy ground truth labels, we developed\nan automated pipeline to bridge the gap between detecting\nAI content in clean conditions and real-world broadcast.\nWe first curated a reference corpus of clean, foreground\n.mp3 tracks for both the AI-generated and human-made\nclasses. We then leveraged an audio fingerprinting system\nto identify the precise occurrences of these tracks within\nthe global broadcast archive. The retrieved segments were\nfiltered using a multi-stage process devised to isolate valid\nclips that satisfied the technical requirements described\nabove, as illustrated in Fig. 1. The following subsections\ndescribe the individual stages of this procedure.\n3.1 Reference Track Selection\nThe construction pipeline begins with a candidate pool of\nclean MP3 reference tracks. Before searching for occurrences of these tracks in TV broadcast content, we categorize them using an ensemble of five AI-music detectors,\nhereafter referred to as the detector ensemble. This configuration promotes methodological diversity by combining\ndetectors trained on different datasets, operating at different sampling rates, and relying on distinct feature representations.\nThe labels were determined separately for both classes.\nFor the human-made class, reference tracks were restricted\nto releases between January 2020 and December 2022,\nensuring that they predated Suno v3.5 and other publicly\navailable or commercial generative music systems capable of producing music of comparable quality. For the AIgenerated class, a candidate track was included only if it\nexceeded the calibrated decision thresholds across all five\ndetectors, requiring unanimous agreement from the ensemble.\nRegarding the models in the detector ensemble. The\nfirst model is a publicly available convolutional neural network presented and assessed in this paper under the name\nCNN Clean. It is based on the architecture proposed by\nAfchar et al. [6], was trained on publicly available data, It\noperates on 5-second audio windows represented as melspectrograms computed from audio resampled to 8 kHz.\nThe model weights and inference code are available in the\nproject repository. For more in-depth implementation details, refer to Section 4.1.\nThe second model is the publicly available SpecTTTra\nalpha-5s detector [13]. It was selected because it achieved\nthe best performance among the evaluated SpecTTTra variants on our control dataset. The model operates on spatiotemporal tokens extracted from audio resampled to 16 kHz\nand was trained on the SONICS suno v3.5 subset.\nThe remaining three models of the detector ensemble\nwere developed for internal research purposes and trained\non in-house data. The third model is a CNN based on the\narchitecture proposed by Afchar et al. [6]. It operates on\n5-second audio windows represented as mel-spectrograms\ncomputed from audio resampled to 16 kHz. The fourth\nmodel is a logistic regression classifier following the ap-\n\n2021\n2022\n2023\n2024\n2025\n\nH1\nH2\nH1\nH2\nH1\nH2\nH1\n\nSuno v3.5 (Summer 2024)\nSuno v4 (Nov 2024)\nSuno v4.5 (May 2025)\nSuno v4.5+ (Jul 2025)\nSuno v5 (Sep 2025)\n\nH2\nH1\nH2\n\n2026 H1\n\n0.0\n\n0.2\n\n0.4\n\n0.6\n\nAI Probability Score\n\n0.8\n\n1.0\n\nFigure 2. Distribution of AI-probability scores produced\nby the detector ensemble for tracks released from 2021 to\n2026. Score values remain low before the release of Suno\nv3.5, and increase afterward, indicating the appearance of\nSuno-generated music.\nproach introduced by Afchar et al. [12]. It operates on\nartifact-fingerprint features extracted from audio resampled to 16 kHz. The fifth model is an MLP trained on\nthe same feature representation as the logistic regression\nclassifier.\nAll five models were trained as binary classifiers under\nclean foreground music conditions to distinguish humanmade from AI-generated tracks (Suno v3.5), and all\nachieved F1 scores above 98%. We performed a conservative calibration on each model to achieve a zero false\npositive rate. The calibration set included 5,000 tracks\nfrom Da-TACOS [20], providing a verified human baseline that predates modern generative models, and a control\nset of 500 AI-generated tracks from the clean subset of AIOpenBMAT. By deriving model-specific decision thresholds that yielded zero errors on the human-composed set,\nwe obtained a labeling criterion with high confidence in\nthe resulting AI-generated samples.\nTo further validate the selection strategy, we analyzed\nthe temporal evolution of AI-positive detections among\nthe artists with the highest numbers of positive cases, as\nshown in Figure 2. Detection scores are consistently low\nbefore the Summer 2024 release date of Suno v3.5, increase markedly after its introduction, and later decrease\nover time. This behavior is consistent with the real-world\nadoption of Suno v3.5 and the subsequent appearance of\nnewer generative models from the same family.\n3.2 Fingerprint & Broadcast Retrieval\nOnce the reference tracks for AI-generated and humanmade classes were defined, we used a private implemen-\n\n\ftation of audio fingerprinting based on landmarks [21] to\nlocate matches in our TV broadcast archive. The matched\nsegments were searched in recordings aired between January 2025 and March 2026 and then extracted directly\nfrom the original TV emissions. These recordings are\narchived at a sampling rate of 8 kHz and a bitrate of\n40 kbps.\n3.3 DMD Filtering\nFollowing fingerprint retrieval, we applied a Deep Music Detector (DMD), based on the approach proposed by\nMeléndez et al. [16]. This model classifies audio segments\nas foreground music, background music, or speech. We\nkept only clips labeled as containing music, whether in the\nforeground or background, thereby removing sound effects\nand incorrect fingerprint matches. These foreground and\nbackground labels are also used when analyzing the performance of AI-generated music detection models (Section 5.3).\n4. EXPERIMENTAL SETUP\nThis benchmark examines how the training domain affects\nCNN-based detectors designed to identify AI-generated\nmusic in real broadcast settings. We compare two variants of the architecture proposed by Afchar et al. [6], both\noperating on 5-second windows but trained under different\nconditions: clean foreground music and broadcast-oriented\ndata. This comparison isolates the effect of the training\ndomain while keeping the model capacity fixed, allowing\nus to examine the limitations of CNN-based AI-generated\nmusic detectors in broadcast environments. The benchmark focuses on Suno v3.5, as model-agnostic detection\nremains an open problem, and this generative model has\npublicly available data and matched reference detectors.\n4.1 Shared Architecture and Preprocessing\nThe CNN-based model has six convolutional layers with\nfilter sizes [16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512] and kernel size 3, followed by average pooling and two fully connected layers.\nIn the pre-processing pipeline, audio is converted to\nmono and resampled to 8 kHz. Then the waveform is\ntransformed into a time-frequency representation using an\nSTFT with a window size of 2048 and a hop length of\n1024. The resulting power spectrogram is projected onto\n128 Mel bands spanning 20 Hz to 4 kHz, converted to the\nlogarithmic dB scale, and standardized with a fixed global\nmean of −4.0 and standard deviation of 3.0. The Melspectrogram is then segmented into discrete time windows\nto form the input tensor of the CNN.\n\n19k songs. For each batch, five 5 s snippets are randomly\nsampled from each track, enabling consistent training.\n4.1.2 CNN Broadcast\nCNN Broadcast uses the same architecture and input representation as CNN Clean, but is trained on data designed to\nemulate broadcast conditions by mixing music and speech.\nThe music sources are the same as for CNN Clean, namely\nFMA-medium for the human-made class and the Suno v3.5\nsubset of SONICS for the AI-generated class. Speech is\ntaken from the 360-hour clean training partition of LibriSpeech [22].\nThe training set is constructed with a controlled ratio of\n70% mixed samples and 30% clean samples for each class.\nIn the mixed condition, speech segments are concatenated\nto span the full duration of each music track and mixed\nwith the music signal at a randomly sampled SNR between\n−30 dB and +30 dB. The resulting files are exported in\nmono at 8 kHz and encoded as .mp3 at 40 kbps before\nentering the shared preprocessing pipeline.\n4.2 Evaluation Scenarios\nTo assess the benchmark models under progressively more\nchallenging conditions, we evaluate them across three scenarios: (i) Clean Foreground Music (CFM), (ii) Synthetic\nTV Broadcast (STB), and (iii) Real TV Broadcast (RTB).\n4.2.1 Clean Foreground Music (CFM).\nFor this scenario, we evaluate the models on the clean music tracks used to build AI-OpenBMAT [7]. The dataset\ncontains 476 human–AI pairs with a sampling rate of\n22.05 kHz and a bitrate of 353 kbps. The AI tracks were\ngenerated from their corresponding human tracks using\nSuno’s extend function. As a result, the pairs are closely\nmatched in musical characteristics such as style, timbre,\npitch, and key. This scenario provides a direct point of\ncomparison with the synthetic broadcast scenario, which\nis built from the same source material.\n4.2.2 Synthetic TV Broadcast (STB).\nWe evaluate the synthetic broadcast scenario using the full\nAI-OpenBMAT dataset, following [7]. The dataset contains 3,294 tracks, totaling 54.9 hours of synthetic broadcast material, with a sampling rate of 22.05 kHz and a bitrate of 353 kbps. These data were created from the same\nclean foreground references described above, using controlled mixtures designed to accurately emulate broadcast\nconditions. This scenario allows us to evaluate model performance under synthetic broadcast conditions while keeping the underlying musical material controlled.\n\n4.1.1 CNN Clean\n\n4.2.3 Real TV Broadcast (RTB).\n\nCNN Clean is trained on clean foreground music only. Audio is converted to mono and resampled to 8 kHz in the\npre-processing pipeline. The human-made class is drawn\nfrom the FMA-medium dataset, comprising approximately\n25k songs, while the AI-generated class is built from the\nSuno v3.5 subset of SONICS, comprising approximately\n\nThe real broadcast scenario uses the full BAMM dataset\nintroduced in this work. It comprises 40 hours of real\nTV broadcast recordings, with approximately 20 hours per\nclass, at a sampling rate of 8 kHz and a bitrate of 40 kbps\nusing AAC-LC encoding. This setting extends previous\nbroadcast-oriented evaluations by shifting from synthetic\n\n\fScenario\n\nCFM\nSTB\nRTB\n\nCNN Clean\n\nCNN Broadcast\n\nF1\n\nROC\n\nF1\n\nROC\n\n0.992\n0.342\n0.186\n\n0.998\n0.909\n0.707\n\n0.992\n0.661\n0.472\n\n0.996\n0.926\n0.775\n\nTable 1. Results across the three evaluation scenarios:\nClean Foreground Music (CFM), Synthetic TV Broadcast\n(STB), and Real TV Broadcast (RTB).\napproximations to real television content, enabling the\nmodels to be tested under the conditions of the target environment.\n5. RESULTS\nWe evaluate both models across three scenarios with increasing levels of broadcast complexity: Clean Foreground Music (CFM), Synthetic TV Broadcast (STB), and\nReal TV Broadcast (RTB). The comparison between CNN\nClean and CNN Broadcast allows us to analyze how the\ntraining domain influences the robustness of AI-generated\nmusic detection. While the CFM scenario provides a controlled evaluation of artifact detection, the STB setup introduces synthetic broadcast conditions through controlled\nmusic–speech mixtures. Finally, the RTB case evaluates\nboth models on the BAMM dataset introduced in this work,\ncapturing the challenges of real television content.\n5.1 Evaluation on Clean Foreground Music\nThe first stage of this evaluation aims to verify whether detectors trained under real-world broadcast constraints, i.e.,\nlow-quality audio at 8 kHz sampling rate, remain reliable\nfor detecting AI-generated music under clean, high-quality\nforeground conditions. As shown in Table 1, both CNN\nClean and CNN Broadcast achieve F1-scores above 99%,\ndemonstrating that the artifacts learned from low-quality\ntraining data are still present in the high-quality data typically used for this task and remain detectable even when\nconsidering only the lower-frequency components of the\naudio spectrum. These results validate the proposed models and provide a baseline for the subsequent evaluations.\n5.2 Evaluation on Synthetic TV Broadcast\nThe synthetic TV broadcast (STB) scenario represents the\nfirst evaluation under mixed-audio conditions, in which\nAI-generated music is no longer isolated but combined\nwith speech. As observed in Table 1, the transition from\nclean foreground music to synthetic broadcast conditions\nproduces a significant reduction in detection performance\nfor both models. The clean-trained model suffers the\ngreatest degradation, demonstrating that artifacts learned\nfrom isolated music do not fully transfer when partially\nmasked by the surrounding broadcast context. This result is consistent with the performance reported for AIOpenBMAT [7] and validates the experimental setup. The\n\nCNN Broadcast model alleviates this degradation by learning from similar mixed conditions, demonstrating that exposure to broadcast-like data improves artifact detection\nunder masking. However, its limited performance suggests\nthat the problem is not only related to the training distribution but also to an intrinsic reduction in artifact visibility\narising from the broadcast scenario’s mixture process.\n5.3 Evaluation on Real TV Broadcast\nThe RTB scenario is the most challenging setting in this\nwork, as it evaluates the detectors directly on real television\ncontent. Unlike STB, which approximates broadcast conditions using controlled synthetic mixtures, RTB includes\nthe full complexity of real recordings. As shown in Table 1, both models perform worse than in STB, indicating\nthat the synthetic setting does not fully capture real broadcast conditions.\nThe CNN Clean model shows the largest degradation,\nconfirming that artifacts learned from isolated foreground\nmusic do not transfer reliably to real broadcasts. In addition to the reduced audio quality of the RTB recordings\n(8 kHz sampling rate and 40 kbps AAC-LC), sound effects, transitions, and diverse mixing strategies can further\nobscure AI-related artifacts.\nFigure 3 shows a detailed comparison of the models’\nperformance when considering BAMM clips with music\nin the background or in the foreground. Both models retain some discriminative ability and perform better on foreground than on background music. This confirms that\nmasking is a major source of degradation. However, the\nlimited performance on foreground segments also indicates\nthat degraded audio quality and other broadcast sounds affect artifact visibility.\nThe score distributions in Figure 4 show that the main\ndifficulty is detecting AI-generated music rather than rejecting human content. While human samples are generally assigned scores near zero, many AI samples also receive low scores, particularly in background music. Overall, broadcast-simulated training improves robustness, but\nit does not fully overcome the effects of real broadcast conditions.\n6. CONCLUSIONS\nIn this paper, we introduced BAMM, a dataset for AIgenerated music detection designed to address key limitations of existing benchmarks. Unlike datasets focused\nprimarily on clean or isolated musical excerpts, BAMM\ntargets the real broadcast setting, where music appears in\nshort segments, alternates between foreground and background roles, and is mixed with speech and sound effects.\nOur experiments show that CNN-based detectors\ntrained on clean foreground music or simulated broadcast\ndata retain some discriminative capacity under controlled\nconditions, but their performance degrades substantially as\nthe evaluation scenario becomes more realistic. In particular, although performance remains limited for both foreground and background music, the models perform slightly\n\n\fAll Clips\n\nBackground music\n\nForeground music\n\n1.0\n\n1.0\n\n0.8\n\n0.8\n\n0.8\n\n0.6\n\n0.6\n\n0.6\n\n0.4\n\n0.4\n\n0.4\n\nTrue Positive Rate\n\n1.0\n\n0.2\n\n0.2\n\n0.2\n\n0.0\n\nCNN Broadcast (AUC=0.775)\nCNN Clean (AUC=0.707)\nRandom\n0.0\n\nCNN Broadcast (AUC=0.858)\nCNN Clean (AUC=0.782)\nRandom\n0.0\n\n0.0\n\n0.2\n\n0.4\n\n0.6\n\n0.8\n\nFalse Positive Rate\n\n1.0\n\n0.0\n\n0.2\n\n0.4\n\n0.6\n\n0.8\n\nFalse Positive Rate\n\n1.0\n\nCNN Broadcast (AUC=0.745)\nCNN Clean (AUC=0.667)\nRandom\n\n0.0\n\n0.2\n\n0.4\n\n0.6\n\n0.8\n\nFalse Positive Rate\n\n1.0\n\nFigure 3. ROC curves for the clean-trained and broadcast-trained CNN models when evaluated on all BAMM clips, only\non foreground music, and only on background music. Performance decreases for background music, showing that detection\nbecomes more difficult when the musical signal is less salient or mixed with other content.\n\nCNN Clean\n1400\n1200\n1000\n800\n600\n400\n200\n0\n\n1200\n\nHuman\nAI\n1000\n\nMusic\nBackground music\n\nHuman\nAI\n\n800\n600\n400\n\nretaría de Estado de Digitalización e Inteligencia\nArtificial, the European Union-Next Generation EU\nfunds and BMAT Music Innovators.\nAnd by the\n\"IMPA\" project (PID2023-152250OB-I00) funded by\nMCIU/AEI/10.13039/501100011033/FEDER, UE.\n\n200\n0\n\nCNN Clean\n\n2500\n\nHuman 2000\nAI\n1750\n\n2000\n1500\n1000\n500\n0 0.0\n\nCNN Broadcast\n\n0.2\n\n0.4\n\n0.6\n\n0.8\n\nProbability (AI)\n\n1500\n1250\n1000\n750\n500\n250\n0 0.0\n1.0\n\nCNN Broadcast\n\n0.2\n\n0.4\n\n0.6\n\n0.8\n\nProbability (AI)\n\n8. REFERENCES\nHuman\nAI\n\n1.0\n\nFigure 4. Score distributions for the CNN models under\nReal TV Broadcast conditions. Blue and red densities denote human-made and AI-generated clips.\nbetter when the music is in the foreground. This suggests that current detectors are not reliably capturing AIgenerated music artifacts, and that the task becomes even\nmore difficult when the musical signal is less salient or embedded within complex audio mixtures.\nThese findings highlight the need for more robust detection methods capable of modeling the acoustic and contextual complexity of real broadcast media. BAMM provides\na benchmark for this direction by exposing failure modes\nnot fully captured by clean-music datasets and encouraging\nthe development of models that can detect AI-generated\nmusic in challenging, real-world broadcast environments.\n\n[1] D. Tencer, “Major record companies sue Suno,\nUdio for ‘mass infringement’ of copyright,”\nhttps://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/majorrecord-companies-sue-ai-music-generators-suno-udiofor-mass-infringement-of-copyright/, Jun. 2024.\n[2] M. Stassen, “Warner Music Group strikes\n‘landmark’ deal with Suno;\nsettles copyright lawsuit against AI music generator,”\nhttps://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/warnermusic-group-settles-with-suno-strikes-first-of-itskind-deal-with-ai-song-generator/, Nov. 2025.\n[3] X. Serra, R. O. Araz, R. Batlle-Roca, L. Juvela,\nD. López, and M. Rocamora, “Technical Solutions\nfor Marking and Detecting AI-generated Audio in\nthe Context of Article 50 of the AI Act,” European Commission, Technical Report, 2026, tender ECCNECT/2024/VLVP/0115 Watermarking Audio.\n[4] J. Wendel, “Ai music tops 50% of daily uploads\non deezer,” https://newsroom-deezer.com/2026/07/\nai-music-exceeds-50-percent-daily-uploads-deezer/,\n2026, [Online; accessed 24-July-2026].\n\n7. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\n\n[5] D. Bolboacă, “First, video killed the radio star, and\nnow AI is going after video: A study on detecting GenAI music in broadcast audio,” https://www.\nbmat.com/genai-music-broadcasting/, 2026, [Online;\naccessed 24-July-2026].\n\nThis work is supported by the \"Cátedra IA y Música\"\nproject (TSI-100929-2023-1), funded by the Sec-\n\n[6] D. Afchar, G. Meseguer-Brocal, and R. Hennequin,\n“AI-generated music detection and its challenges,” in\n\n\fProc. of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 2025.\n[7] D. López-Ayala, A. Cabello, P. Zinemanas, E. Molina,\nand M. Rocamora, “AI-generated music detection in\nbroadcast monitoring,” in Proc. of the 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\nProcessing (ICASSP), 2026.\n[8] Y. Li, M. Milling, L. Specia, and B. W. Schuller, “From\nAudio Deepfake Detection to AI-Generated Music Detection – a pathway and overview,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2412.00571, 2024.\n[9] D. Salvi, H. V. Koops, and E. Quinton, “Not All Deepfakes Are Created Equal: Triaging Audio Forgeries for\nRobust Deepfake Singer Identification,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2510.17474, 2025.\n[10] A. Défossez, J. Copet, G. Synnaeve, and Y. Adi, “High\nfidelity neural audio compression,” Transactions on\nMachine Learning Research (TMLR), 2023.\n[11] R. Kumar, P. Seetharaman, A. Luebs, I. Kumar, and\nK. Kumar, “High-fidelity audio compression with improved RVQGAN,” in Proc. of the Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS), 2023.\n[12] D. Afchar, G. Meseguer-Brocal, K. Akesbi, and\nR. Hennequin, “A Fourier Explanation of AI-music\nArtifacts,” in Proceedings of the International Society\nfor Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR),\n2025.\n[13] M. A. Rahman, Z. I. A. Hakim, N. H. Sarker, B. Paul,\nand S. A. Fattah, “Sonics: Synthetic or not - identifying counterfeit songs,” in International Conference on\nLearning Representations (ICLR), 2025.\n[14] C.\nGDCJ,\n“Genius\nsong\nlyrics,”\nhttps:\n//www.kaggle.com/datasets/carlosgdcj/\ngenius-song-lyrics-with-language-information,\n2023, [Online; accessed 16-March-2026].\n[15] L. C. Vila, B. L. T. Sturm, L. Casini, and D. Dalmazzo, “The AI Music Arms Race: On the Detection of AI-Generated Music,” Transactions of the International Society for Music Information Retrieval\n(TISMIR), vol. 8, no. 1, pp. 179–194, 2025.\n[16] B. Meléndez Catalán, “Relative music loudness estimation in TV broadcast audio using deep learning: An\nindustrial perspective,” Ph.D. dissertation, Universitat\nPompeu Fabra, Apr. 2021.\n[17] B. Meléndez-Catalán, E. Molina, and E. Gómez,\n“Open broadcast media audio from tv: A dataset of\ntv broadcast audio with relative music loudness annotations,” Transactions of the International Society for\nMusic Information Retrieval (TISMIR), vol. 2, no. 1,\npp. 43–51, 2019.\n\n[18] G. Cortès-Sebastià, M. Miron, E. Molina, A. Ciurana,\nand X. Serra, “Enhanced television broadcast monitoring with source separation-assisted audio fingerprinting: A case study,” Multimedia Tools and Applications,\nvol. 84, no. 42, pp. 50 595–50 628, 2025.\n[19] G. Cortès, A. Ciurana, E. Molina, M. Miron, O. Meyers, J. Six, and X. Serra, “BAF: An audio fingerprinting dataset for broadcast monitoring,” in Proc. of the\nInternational Society for Music Information Retrieval\nConference (ISMIR), 2022.\n[20] F. Yesiler, C. Tralie, A. Correya, D. F. Silva, P. Tovstogan, E. Gómez, and X. Serra, “Da-TACOS: A dataset\nfor cover song identification and understanding,” in\nProc. of the International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2019.\n[21] G. Cortès Sebastià, “Music identification with audio\nfingerprinting an industrial perspective,” Ph.D. dissertation, Universitat Pompeu Fabra, Feb. 2025.\n[22] M. Korvas, O. Plátek, O. Dušek, L. Žilka, and F. Jurčíček, “Free English and Czech telephone speech corpus shared under the CC-BY-SA 3.0 license,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Language\nResources and Evaluation (LREC), 2014.\n\n\f", "numLines": 732, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 732}}}
